window.__NOTE_CONTENT = "<h2 id=\"the-natural-theory-of-law\">THE NATURAL THEORY OF LAW.</h2>\n<p>Natural law in its simplest understanding and definition refers to the “Un written law”</p>\n<p>that is more or less the same for everyone everywhere. It is also concept of body of</p>\n<p>principles that is common to all mankind and generally posited, recognizable by</p>\n<p>human reasonability (human conscience).</p>\n<p>Natural law as a philosophical theory states that humans have got certain rights, moral</p>\n<p>values and responsibilities which are inherent in human nature. This theory” natural</p>\n<p>law” is based on the idea that laws are universal concepts which are uniform and not</p>\n<p>based on culture or custom. Human beings are not taught natural law; they initiate it</p>\n<p>by making good and right decisions. Therefore, it is said to be discoverable through</p>\n<p>the exercise of reason. 1</p>\n<p>The conceptualization of natural law is basically dictate of reason, thinking mind and</p>\n<p>sometimes called “Conscience”. It is law discovered by human reason. Natural law is</p>\n<p>also understood as “God made law” which we must conform if we are to be</p>\n<p>regarded as natural law.</p>\n<p>With 2,500years of history, it is not surprising that the question “what is natural law”</p>\n<p>provokes so many different answers from different scholars with philosophical</p>\n<p>understanding. The expression natural law has continuity but this does not</p>\n<p>mean that the concept has remained static. It has had very different stages,</p>\n<p>meanings and has served entirely different purposes. D’Entreves points out “Many of</p>\n<p>the ambiguities of the concept of natural law that, it must be ascribed to the ambiguity</p>\n<p>of the concept of nature that underlies it.” 2 Thus, the concept of natural law has been</p>\n<p>defined by many scholars or authors accordingly as accessed below: - St Thomas</p>\n<p>Aquinas (1225-1274), natural law is that part of eternal law</p>\n<p>1</p>\n<p>What              is          Natural            Law?                 &lt;</p>\n<p>https://corporatefinanceinstitute.com/resources/esg/natural-law/&gt;            accessed</p>\n<p>15th.january.2023.</p>\n<p>2</p>\n<p>Natural law (Rev.ed.,1970), pg.16</p>\n<p>1</p>\n<p>of God (“The reason of divine wisdom”) which is known by human beings by means</p>\n<p>of their powers of reason and association with God. Aquinas puts that “Human or</p>\n<p>positive law is the application of natural law to particular social circumstances. He</p>\n<p>believes that positive law that violates natural law is not true law. He further asserts</p>\n<p>that; natural law theory is a new basis in human reason.</p>\n<p>St Thomas Aquinas further thought to advance the systematization of Catholic</p>\n<p>doctrine in which law was viewed as an aspect of God’s plan for mankind. Natural law</p>\n<p>did not begin with Aquinas; he built upon principles formulated by Greek and Roman</p>\n<p>scholars (who had written of the immutable unwritten laws of heaven), including</p>\n<p>Aristotle, who had differentiated the naturally just from conventionally just and Cicero</p>\n<p>who had proclaimed the true and primary law – the right reason of the High God.</p>\n<p>Within the Catholic church, natural law doctrines had been shaped by scholars such as</p>\n<p>the following;</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-augustine-354-430-one-of-the-early-fathers-of-the-church-taught\">1. Augustine (354-430). One of the early Fathers of the Church, taught</h3>\n<p>that absolute ideal laws of nature had been evident in the golden age</p>\n<p>of mankind which had preceded the fall of man. That age had seen</p>\n<p>man in a state of innocence and justice, he lived under the guidance</p>\n<p>of the rules of reason. After his fall, the absolute law of nature could</p>\n<p>no longer be realized and as a result, human law together with</p>\n<p>property and institutions had appeared.</p>\n<p>Positive (man-made) law must strive so as to assist man in fulfilling</p>\n<p>the command of God’s eternal law. Thus the church must strive total</p>\n<p>sovereignty over the state.</p>\n<p>If worldly law conflicts with the eternal, natural law the provisions of the</p>\n<p>world law should be ignored. World law which was unjust could not be</p>\n<p>law</p>\n<p>2</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-isidore-560-636-reiterated-augustine-s-teachings-on-the-law-and\">2. Isidore (560-636) reiterated Augustine’s teachings on the law and</h3>\n<p>taught that only those rulers who acted in a spirit of justice ought to</p>\n<p>command the respect of their subjects.</p>\n<p>He said that natural law is common to all peoples in that it is possessed by</p>\n<p>an instinct of nature, not by any human agreement, as marriage, the</p>\n<p>begetting and raring of children, the common possession of all things, the</p>\n<p>universal freedom of all among others. For this or whatever is like this</p>\n<p>could never constitute and injustice but must be considered in accordance</p>\n<p>with natural equity.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-gratian-1140-a-benedictine-published-a-collection-of-texts-in\">3. Gratian (1140), a Benedictine, published a collection of texts in</h3>\n<p>which he noted that the law of nature was considered as the law of</p>\n<p>God. If a custom were to be recognized as valid law it would have to</p>\n<p>conform to the golden rule of the Gospel (All things whatsoever ye</p>\n<p>would that men should do to you, do you even so to them).</p>\n<p>Statutes, secular and ecclesiastical, were to be rejected totally if shown to be</p>\n<p>contrary to natural law.</p>\n<p>Aquinas’ concept of the law</p>\n<p>The writings of Aquinas on law, recorded largely in the summa Theological,</p>\n<p>Part 11 9C 1266), have dominated the thinking of the Catholic jurists for</p>\n<p>centuries. The contemporary emergence of the Neo-Scholastic movement</p>\n<p>testifies to the enduring influence of the remarkable synthesis of law</p>\n<p>achieved by Aquinas.</p>\n<p>Fundamental to the synthesis is a set of precisely enunciated beliefs.</p>\n<p>God is the highest good and from him all things emerge.</p>\n<p>The order of nature which we can observe and analyze is God’s creation,</p>\n<p>and because order presupposes reason, there is some transcendent reason</p>\n<p>behind the creation.</p>\n<p>3</p>\n<p>Law and Divine Reason</p>\n<p>Law in all its manifestations flows ultimately from Divine Reason.</p>\n<h4 id=\"aquinas-argued\">Aquinas argued:</h4>\n<p>Law is a rule and measure of acts, whereby man is induced to act or is</p>\n<p>restrained from acting, now the rule and measure of human acts is the</p>\n<p>reason, which is the first principle of human acts, consequently it follows</p>\n<p>that law is something pertaining to reason.</p>\n<p>Man’s laws are ordinances of reason for the common good made by him who has the</p>\n<p>care of the community and promulgated.</p>\n<p>Thomas Aquinas3 four fold classification of law (1224-1274) and these forms</p>\n<p>include: -</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-the-eternal-law-lex-aeterna\">1. The Eternal law (Lex aeterna).</h3>\n<p>It’s the law only known to God who rationally directs all creatures. All creatures are</p>\n<p>ruled by the law but the implication of man’s possession of free will is his capacity to</p>\n<p>disobey or act contrary to this law. Therefore, by this law, unreasoning things must</p>\n<p>obey it, but man who has free will has the capacity to disobey.</p>\n<p>The eternal law in its totality is known to God only but some blessed persons may</p>\n<p>perceive it in its full truth. The broad general principle that mirror God’s intentions</p>\n<p>for mankind comprise the natural law.</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-the-divine-law-lex-divina\">2. The Divine law (Lex Divina).</h3>\n<p>This denotes to the law revealed in scriptures (Bible). It comes into plan when law</p>\n<p>discovered by human reason fails by helping to clear doubts in the mind of man as</p>\n<p>to the law of nature. In this regard it’s taken as a</p>\n<p>3</p>\n<p>St. Thomas Aquinas&lt; https://www.britannica.com/biography/Saint-Thomas-</p>\n<p>Aquinas&gt; accessed 15th.january.2023.</p>\n<p>4</p>\n<p>guide for human reason and can be revealed to selected few prophets. For example,</p>\n<p>in the bible, God revealed certain laws or rules of conduct to the people of Israel</p>\n<p>through prophets like; Isaiah, Jeremiah, Ezekiel and among others.</p>\n<p>As reveled in scriptures, although not identical with natural law, it is not contrary to it.</p>\n<p>Divine revelation – God’s law provides a guide for man’s reason thus allowing his</p>\n<p>nature to be perfected by Divine grace.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-the-natural-law-lex-naturalis\">3. The Natural law (Lex Naturalis).</h3>\n<p>This connotes to the law that manifests man’s participation in the working of cosmic</p>\n<p>law or the law governing the universe through the medium of reason. It’s therefore</p>\n<p>that small voice in man’s heart and the small god which exposes the difference</p>\n<p>between good and evil and encourages one to do the former and to refrain from the</p>\n<p>latter.</p>\n<p>Everything seeks the preservation of its own being according to its nature. By reason</p>\n<p>of this inclination whatever is a means of preserving human life and awarding off its</p>\n<p>obstacles, belongs to the natural law.</p>\n<h3 id=\"4-the-human-lex-humana\">4. The Human (Lex Humana).</h3>\n<p>This refers to the “manmade” law and can be termed as positive law though it</p>\n<p>incorporates portion of natural law. This is based on the fact that, man’s participation</p>\n<p>in eternal law enables one to have wisdom to enact positive law for the governance,</p>\n<p>regulation of human conduct in society. Such laws may or may not conform to the</p>\n<p>law of God (Natural law) and where it conforms, it is said to harmonize with the</p>\n<p>divine will of creator and where it does not, it’s said to fail the test of natural law or</p>\n<p>denied legality.</p>\n<p>It is prudent to note that, the principles of natural law as expounded by Aquinas have</p>\n<h4 id=\"been-placed-by-commentators-in-three-groups\">been placed by commentators in three groups:</h4>\n<h3 id=\"1-the-supreme-group-demands-love-for-god-and-one-s-neighbors-the-doing\">1. The supreme group, demands love for God and one’s neighbors, the doing</h3>\n<p>of good and the avoiding of evil.</p>\n<p>5</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-the-second-group-based-on-the-first-group-and-reiterating-the-principles-of\">2. The second group, based on the first group and reiterating the principles of</h3>\n<p>the Ten Commandments.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-the-third-group-which-is-constituted-by-the-rules-of-justice-resulting-from-the\">3. The third group, which is constituted by the rules of justice resulting from the</h3>\n<p>application of principles in the first and second groups to particular situations.</p>\n<p>When is a law not law?</p>\n<p>Aquinas argued that, in the light of natural principles, laws framed by men are</p>\n<p>either just or unjust. A law is not law merely because it is decreed by a</p>\n<p>sovereign.</p>\n<p>Where a law has the appropriate moral dimension and is ordained to the</p>\n<p>common good, it is a just law. Hence, where it conforms to natural law it is</p>\n<p>just.</p>\n<p>Unjust laws</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-a-law-which-is-contrary-to-human-good-either-in-respect-of-its-form\">1. A law which is contrary to human good either in respect of its form</h3>\n<p>or end, is no law at all.</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-a-law-which-is-opposed-to-the-divine-good-such-as-the-law-of-a\">2. A law which is opposed to the Divine good, such as the law of a</h3>\n<p>tyrant inducing to idolatry (and therefore, a violation of the natural</p>\n<p>law), must be disregarded, because it is better to obey God than man.</p>\n<p>Significance of Aquinas’ view of natural law</p>\n<p>Natural law demands the elevation of human reason. God’s purposes maybe</p>\n<p>discerned in part by reason. God’s law is the reason of his wisdom, and human laws</p>\n<p>and institutions are best-founded when built upon reason.</p>\n<p>However, not all Catholic scholars in the time of Aquinas accepted his</p>\n<p>conclusions/Criticisms of Aquinas view of natural law</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-john-duns-scotus-1265-1308-a-franciscan-scholar-argued-that-in\">1. John Duns Scotus (1265-1308), a Franciscan scholar, argued that, in</h3>\n<p>relation to natural law, reason was not superior to will. Man’s will, not</p>\n<p>his reason, guides his intellectual insight.</p>\n<p>6</p>\n<p>The single principle of natural law is to love God; other principles of</p>\n<p>human behavior are matters for decision within man-made law.</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-william-of-occam-1290-1349-suggested-that-philosophical\">2. William of Occam (1290-1349), suggested that philosophical</h3>\n<p>speculation be confined to the observable. The modification by man-</p>\n<p>made law of principles deduced from natural law was permissible and</p>\n<p>was not, as Aquinas seemed to suggest, contrary to reason and Divine</p>\n<p>law.</p>\n<p>John Finnis,</p>\n<p>Finnis a British Scholar (1929) is a Catholic scholar who seeks to investigate the</p>\n<p>relevance of natural law to contemporary society. In his Natural Law and Human</p>\n<p>Rights (1980) he restates aspects of natural law doctrine in novel form.</p>\n<p>Morality and law are given a rational basis which rests upon two principles.</p>\n<p>First, there are certain basic values of human existence (human goods) which</p>\n<p>are self-evident. They may be secured only through the law and its institutions.</p>\n<p>Secondly, the theory of natural law is the identification of practical right-</p>\n<p>mindedness/reasonableness, of good and proper order among men is required</p>\n<p>to achieve human goods and this necessitates the existence of an appropriate system</p>\n<p>of law and in individual conduct.</p>\n<p>It is worth to note that, Finnis definition consists of two (2) concepts that is: there</p>\n<p>are certain basic values that are good for human beings and secondly, the</p>\n<p>requirement of practical reasonableness (human conscience).</p>\n<p>Under natural law and Legal system,</p>\n<p>7</p>\n<p>Finnis keeps in mind the nature, purpose and ultimate significance of society. He</p>\n<p>notes that jurists must keep in mind the need to bring definition, clarity and</p>\n<p>predictability into the reactions of members of society, and this requires organized</p>\n<p>rules and institutions.</p>\n<p>Human well-being and the flourishing of the individual require a legal system which</p>\n<p>will exemplify the rule of law as derived from the natural law. The rules of such a</p>\n<p>system would ensure clear, coherent and stable regulations, accountability of those</p>\n<p>who administer the law, and administration carried out in a manner which is seen to</p>\n<p>be consistent with promulgated principles.</p>\n<p>The concept of human goods.</p>\n<p>Finnis set out a catalogue of forms of “human flourishing” that is, the requirements</p>\n<p>of individual who are to attain their potential. Seven such irreducibly basic goods are</p>\n<p>enumerated although there are other forms of human goods and these are</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-life\">1. Life</h3>\n<p>Life is in accordance with traditional principles of natural law – a basic value</p>\n<p>which corresponds to the human being’s drive for self- preservation. Life is</p>\n<p>used by Finnis as signifying every aspect of the vitality which puts a human</p>\n<p>being in good shape for self- determination. The realization of such a basic</p>\n<p>human purpose involves the existence of a functioning system of law.</p>\n<p>Finnis notes that all human societies appear to show a significant measure of</p>\n<p>concern for the value of life and none allows killing without a fairly definite</p>\n<p>justification.</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-knowledge\">2. Knowledge</h3>\n<p>Speculative knowledge with which Finnis is concerned, comprises knowledge</p>\n<p>sought for its own sake rather than in pursuit of some other objective. It will</p>\n<p>embody the drive of curiosity and range from scientific enquiry to mundane</p>\n<p>questioning.</p>\n<p>8</p>\n<p>Truth as the end of knowledge is worth pursuing.</p>\n<p>The principle that knowledge is good to be persuaded is according to Finnis,</p>\n<p>self-evident and its value as a guide to action requires no further principle to</p>\n<p>support it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-play\">3. Play</h3>\n<p>A large and irreducible element of human culture involving engaging in</p>\n<p>performances which have no point beyond the performance itself, enjoyed for</p>\n<p>its sake.</p>\n<h3 id=\"4-aesthetic-experience\">4. Aesthetic experience</h3>\n<p>This need not involve an action of one’s own, it maybe the inner experience of</p>\n<p>say, an appreciation of beauty.</p>\n<h3 id=\"5-sociability\">5. Sociability</h3>\n<p>This involves friendship. To be in a relationship of friendship with at least one</p>\n<p>other person is a fundamental form of good.</p>\n<h3 id=\"6-practical-reasonableness\">6. Practical reasonableness</h3>\n<p>This basic good involves bringing one’s intelligence to bear effectively on</p>\n<p>problems related to the choosing of one’s actions and life style and molding</p>\n<p>one’s character.</p>\n<h3 id=\"7-religion\">7. Religion</h3>\n<p>Finnis gives this term a wide meaning – recognition (however residual) of and</p>\n<p>concern about, an order of things beyond each and every man.</p>\n<p>Justice and liberty. Finnis stated that justice requires due respect for liberty</p>\n<p>which while itself not one of the basic human goods, is a necessary condition</p>\n<p>for the active human realization of any of those goods.</p>\n<p>The concept of practical reasonableness.</p>\n<p>Finnis classifies under ten (10) headings the essential features of practical</p>\n<p>reasonableness (which features also as a human good).</p>\n<p>9</p>\n<p>These headings encapsulate principles which seem to be in the spirit of the</p>\n<p>classical natural law and these are;</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-if-the-individual-is-to-distinguish-moral-right-actions-from-wrong-actions-he\">1. If the individual is to distinguish moral right actions from wrong actions, he</h3>\n<p>must have a rational life span, a harmonious set of orientations, purposes</p>\n<p>and commitments.</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-he-must-pay-equal-regard-to-each-of-the-basic-human-goods\">2. He must pay equal regard to each of the basic human goods.</h3>\n<h3 id=\"3-he-must-not-leave-out-of-account-the-necessity-for-others-to\">3. He must not leave out of account the necessity for others to</h3>\n<p>participate in these goods.</p>\n<h3 id=\"4-he-must-not-attribute-to-any-project-the-overriding-significance-of-a-basic\">4. He must not attribute to any project the overriding significance of a basic</h3>\n<p>human good.</p>\n<h3 id=\"5-he-must-pursue-his-general-commitments-with-creativity-and-must-not\">5. He must pursue his general commitments with creativity and must not</h3>\n<h3 id=\"6-he-must-not-waste-his-opportunities-by-acting-inefficiently\">6. He must not waste his opportunities by acting inefficiently.</h3>\n<h3 id=\"7-he-must-not-choose-directly-against-human-good\">7. He must not choose directly against human good.</h3>\n<h3 id=\"8-he-must-foster-the-common-good-of-his-community\">8. He must foster the common good of his community.</h3>\n<h3 id=\"9-he-must-not-act-contrary-to-his-conscience\">9. He must not act contrary to his conscience.</h3>\n<h3 id=\"10-he-must-not-choose-apparent-goods-which-he-knows-to-be-merely\">10. He must not choose apparent goods which he knows to be merely</h3>\n<p>the stimulations of real goods.</p>\n<p>Unjust laws</p>\n<p>Finnis stands within the tradition of the natural law in his consideration of just and</p>\n<p>unjust laws. For purposes of assessing one’s legal obligations in the moral sense the</p>\n<p>individual may exercise his right to discount those laws which are unjust.</p>\n<p>Laws of this type do not possess the moral authority which ought to stem from their</p>\n<p>own or formal source but unjust laws are not a nullity however do not meet the</p>\n<p>demands of natural law.</p>\n<p>Finnis and natural rights</p>\n<p>Finnis refers in his text to certain absolute human claim rights and these include;</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-a-right-of-an-individual-not-to-have-his-life-taken-directly-as-a-means-to\">1. A right of an individual not to have his life taken directly as a means to</h3>\n<p>any further end.</p>\n<p>10</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-a-right-not-to-be-positively-lied-to-in-situations-in-which-one\">2. A right not to be positively lied to in situations in which one</h3>\n<p>expects reasonable factual information.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-a-right-not-to-be-condemned-on-charges-on-charges-which-are-known\">3. A right not to be condemned on charges on charges which are known</h3>\n<p>to be false.</p>\n<h3 id=\"4-a-right-not-to-be-deprived-of-procreative-capacities\">4. A right not to be deprived of procreative capacities.</h3>\n<h3 id=\"5-the-right-to-be-considered-in-any-assessment-of-what-is-required-in-the\">5. The right to be considered in any assessment of what is required in the</h3>\n<p>name of the common good of society.</p>\n<p>Criticisms of Finnis</p>\n<p>Criticisms of Finnis have drawn attention to the very wide nature of the</p>\n<p>categories of human goods, rendering analysis of knowledge, sociability,</p>\n<p>extremely difficult.</p>\n<p>His classification of religion as a human good gives rise to unusual problems of</p>\n<p>interpretation. Some jurists see in the list of human goods yet another highly-</p>\n<p>subjectively catalogue of principles which most religions have advocated as</p>\n<p>constituting paths to virtue.</p>\n<p>Hume (1711-1776) holds that our knowledge of matters of fact can be derived</p>\n<p>solely from the data of experience. He further holds that natural law is real only</p>\n<p>in the sense that some individual entertain a feeling that it exists. Its truths</p>\n<p>cannot be asserted meaningfully, nor can they be demonstrated in acceptable</p>\n<p>fashion. Its attempts to derive ought from is are meaningless.</p>\n<p>Benthan (1748-1831) attached natural law as nothing but a phrase ‘nonsense</p>\n<p>upon stilts’. Additionally, the spread of the doctrine of natural law would</p>\n<p>undermine the fabric of sovereignty within a community.</p>\n<p>Rose (1899-1979) a Danish jurist delivers a sustained attack on the entire</p>\n<p>conceptual basis of natural law;</p>\n<p>• The metaphysical speculation underlying natural law is totally beyond the</p>\n<p>reach of verification, such speculation belongs to the childhood of</p>\n<p>civilization</p>\n<p>11</p>\n<p>• Natural law has been used to defend slavery and fraternity. How may</p>\n<p>one make a valid choice between these absolutes except by absolute</p>\n<p>assertion elevated above all rational argumentation?</p>\n<p>• The ultimate basis for every so-called natural right is the individual’s</p>\n<p>private and direct insight, i.e., intuition. It cannot be otherwise and one</p>\n<p>person’s intuition is as good as that of another.</p>\n<p>Neo Scholasticism and the natural law</p>\n<p>Today’s Neo-Scholastic movement in the field of natural law is concerned with the</p>\n<p>further development and adoption of the teachings of Aquinas on law.</p>\n<p>It is important to note that not all members of this movement accept the theology of</p>\n<p>Aquinas: thus, Adler (1902) a prominent American advocate of a contemporary</p>\n<p>natural law and a non-Catholic, notes six (6) widely- held principles derived</p>\n<h4 id=\"directly-from-aquinas-which-have-unusual-relevance-for-law-today\">directly from Aquinas, which have unusual relevance for law today:</h4>\n<h3 id=\"1-government-made-laws-do-not-constitute-the-sole-directions-of-human\">1. Government made laws do not constitute the sole directions of human</h3>\n<p>conduct which ought to apply to persons within society.</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-there-can-be-discovered-rules-and-principles-with-application-to-all-persons\">2. There can be discovered rules and principles with application to all persons</h3>\n<p>in all types of society.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-general-principles-of-conduct-exist-outside-man-made-rules-of-law\">3. General principles of conduct exist outside man-made rules of law</h3>\n<h3 id=\"4-these-principles-will-emerge-through-man-s-exercise-of-his-reason\">4. These principles will emerge through man’s exercise of his reason</h3>\n<h3 id=\"5-general-principles-resulting-from-man-s-reason-should-be-the-source-of-all\">5. General principles, resulting from man’s reason, should be the source of all</h3>\n<p>particular rules of conduct.</p>\n<p>12</p>\n<p>Adler further advocates that attention be paid to the following principles;</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-that-the-positive-man-made-law-should-not-constitute-the-sole-direction-of\">1. That the positive (man-made) law should not constitute the sole direction of</h3>\n<p>discovered</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-that-universal-rules-of-conduct-can-be-discovered\">2. That universal rules of conduct can be discovered</h3>\n<h3 id=\"3-that-general-principles-of-conduct-emerging-from-man-s-exercise-of-his-reason\">3. That general principles of conduct emerging from man’s exercise of his reason</h3>\n<p>can provide touchstones in order to judge the validity and worth of the rules of</p>\n<p>the law.</p>\n<p>The 17th century Dutch jurist Hugo Grotius believes that humans by nature are not</p>\n<p>only reasonable but social. Thus, the rules that are natural to them are dictated to</p>\n<p>them by reason alone and enable them to live in harmony with one another.</p>\n<p>Salmond puts that, it’s the idea and law that consists of rules in accordance with</p>\n<p>reason and nature. He accords natural law to ordinary human law that’s truly law in</p>\n<p>so far as it conforms to the principles of natural justice and morality that constitute</p>\n<p>natural law. This is envisaged on the fact that man should live according to nature</p>\n<p>since the distinctive feature of man’s nature is his endowment with reason and</p>\n<p>therefore live to the dictates of reason.</p>\n<p>Salmond further inscribes natural law as branch of law that’s described as the law of</p>\n<p>nature, higher law, eternal and divine law.</p>\n<p>Cicero a Roman Orator, states that, true law is a right reason in agreement with</p>\n<p>nature whose appreciation is “universal, unchanging and everlasting.” It is a sin</p>\n<p>to try to alter this law, nor is it allowable to attempt to repeal any part of it and</p>\n<p>impossible to abolish it entirely.</p>\n<p>13</p>\n<p>Cicero further propounds that, “we need not to look outside ourselves for an</p>\n<p>expounder or interpreter of it”. And there will not be different laws at Rome and at</p>\n<p>Athens or at future but one external and unchangeable law will be valid for all nations</p>\n<p>and all times and there will be one master and ruler (God) over us all for, He’s the</p>\n<p>author of this law, the promulgator and its enforcement as the judge. Whoever</p>\n<p>disobeys it denies his/her human nature, and by reason of this fact, he will suffer the</p>\n<p>worst penalties, even if he escapes what’s commonly considered punishment.4</p>\n<p>Burlamqui in his principles of natural law 1751, emphasized that, natural law</p>\n<p>comprises of rules which necessarily agree with nature and state of man. That, without</p>\n<p>observing their maxims, the peace and happiness of society can never be preserved.</p>\n<p>They are called natural laws because knowledge of them may be attained merely by</p>\n<p>the light of reason, from the act of their essential agreeableness with the constitution</p>\n<p>of human nature but rather laws founded upon the general constitution of human</p>\n<p>nature and only based upon the will of God.</p>\n<p>Radbruch a positivist in his legal philosophy 1945 said, “these are principles of</p>\n<p>law that are stronger than any statute. Any laws conflicting with its principles is devoid</p>\n<p>of its validity.” Natural law theories are basically theological as they are all alluded to</p>\n<p>God. These theories rely on allusion that, the universe is being founded and ruled by</p>\n<p>some deity (God) who created principles by which the universe inhabited by human</p>\n<p>beings is ordered and regulated.</p>\n<p>Its from these principles that, the morals or conscience of humanity derive</p>\n<p>because natural law exists in rational human beings who are created by God by</p>\n<p>possessing rational ideas, capacity to know what is good and bad. They have the</p>\n<p>intellectual capacity without assistance of another person to discover natural law. This</p>\n<p>is contrally to secular law where one is guided by his/ her ensuing knowledge to be</p>\n<p>in position to order his life</p>\n<p>4</p>\n<p>Cicero&#x27;s Teaching on Natural Law&lt; https://www.hillsdale.edu/wp-</p>\n<p>content/uploads/2017/07/1981-    Ciceros_Teaching_on_Natural_Law.pdf&gt;</p>\n<p>th</p>\n<p>accessed 15 .january.2023.</p>\n<p>14</p>\n<p>according to his choice in a moral way or an immoral manner. Thus, on extreme note,</p>\n<p>Hugo Grotius stated “There would be natural law even if there were no God.”</p>\n<p>Stammler (1856-1938), a German jurist, was concerned with the formulation of a</p>\n<p>right law based upon justice. He argues that each era requires its own right law which</p>\n<p>should co-exist with its positive law.</p>\n<p>The rules of the community’s positive law should be evaluated, so as to justify their</p>\n<p>use, by reference to standards derived from the prevalent ethical and philosophical</p>\n<p>doctrines, and a law found to be deficient would be corrected by the courts or the</p>\n<p>legislature.</p>\n<h4 id=\"stammler-set-out-his-views-as-follows\">Stammler set out his views as follows:</h4>\n<h3 id=\"1-the-jurist-s-task-is-to-assist-in-the-function-of-providing-the-law-with-a-quality-of\">1. The jurist’s task is to assist in the function of providing the law with a quality of</h3>\n<p>objective justice through the frequent appraisal of empirically-conditioned legal</p>\n<p>rules.</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-law-is-a-vital-aspect-of-man-s-social-existence-embodying-in-an-important\">2. Law is a vital aspect of man’s social existence, embodying, in an important</h3>\n<p>sense, the collective will of the community and binding citizens in a unity of</p>\n<p>purpose.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-man-must-be-treated-as-an-end-in-himself-not-as-the-mere-object-of-an-arbitrary\">3. Man must be treated as an end in himself, not as the mere object of an arbitrary</h3>\n<p>will.</p>\n<h3 id=\"4-the-exercise-of-reason-is-essential-for-the-understanding-and-acceptance-of\">4. The exercise of reason is essential for the understanding and acceptance of</h3>\n<p>one’s responsibilities to other members of society.</p>\n<h3 id=\"5-a-society-in-flux-requires-no-modification-of-the-essential-idea-of-law-what-is\">5. A society in flux requires no modification of the essential idea of law, what is</h3>\n<p>needed is a modification of rules in accordance with the realities of that society</p>\n<p>–essentially, a natural law with a variable content.</p>\n<p>Historical evolution of natural law.</p>\n<p>15</p>\n<p>In tracing the evolution of natural law, it’s worth to be discerned of conception of</p>\n<p>natural law among almost all people.5 It’s nevertheless to the Greeks that we must first</p>\n<p>look6. Whether the law of nature was first conceived as governing the cosmic and later</p>\n<p>only applied to man or society or as (Kelsen contends) that the idea of universal law</p>\n<p>was projection of the law of the state into the physical universe, may well be disputed.</p>\n<p>The Greeks though never interested in the technical development of law, they were</p>\n<p>much concerned in exploring its philosophical foundations and in doing so, evolved</p>\n<p>many fundamental concepts of which natural law was the most important.</p>\n<p>The most important scholars are; St. Thomas Aquinas, (1225-1274) who had to later</p>\n<p>formulate from Aristotle and Cicero. Other catholic scholars like of Augustine</p>\n<p>(354-430), Isidore 560-636 also preceded him.</p>\n<p>These catholic scholars believed that indeed law was inseparable from God, morality</p>\n<p>and ethics. These believed that indeed the law was Devine and manifested from GOD</p>\n<p>through his messengers (prophets) and the holy books such the bible.</p>\n<p>The evolution of natural law can be deduced from the decline of the city- state and</p>\n<p>rise of the large empires in the Greek world, that the natural laws as a universal system</p>\n<p>comes to the fore. Until the stoics “nature”.7 Had meant the “order of things”, This</p>\n<p>came to be identified with man’s reason. When man lived according to “reason”, he</p>\n<p>was living “naturally” as this contained universal force. The best representative of this</p>\n<p>thinking is Roman orator, Cicero8. His definition of natural law as a “right reason in</p>\n<p>agreement with nature” has been enormously influential. He was the first to contend</p>\n<p>for the striking down of positive laws which contravened the natural law.</p>\n<p>5</p>\n<p>(Lloyde, The idea of law (1985), pp 48-51. For the influence of Hebrew sources</p>\n<p>particularly the Noahite code- on selden, see M. crowe (1987) 12 Natural law forum 184</p>\n<p>court.</p>\n<p>6 on the Greek contribution see L.weinreb, Natural law and justice, Chap. 1 (1987).</p>\n<p>7</p>\n<p>for the different meanings of this for the stoics, see finnis, op, cit, pg 374-377.</p>\n<p>8 See post, 137 and see Weinreb op, cit n, 12, p 39-41.</p>\n<p>16</p>\n<p>Medieval Period/ ancient</p>\n<p>This era was championed by theology of the catholic church which set the tone and</p>\n<p>patterns of all speculative though. Two (2) vital principles animated medieval thought.</p>\n<p>Namely; Unity derived from God and involving one faith, one church, empire and secondly</p>\n<p>supremacy of the law not merely man-made but rather conceived as part of the universe9.</p>\n<p>During this era, Aquinas was bedeviled by the notion of law and human dominion</p>\n<p>being rooted in sin. It was not until Aristotelian philosophy and catholic faith in a</p>\n<p>universal divine, he rejected the idea that civil government was necessarily tainted with</p>\n<p>original sin and argued for the existence of a hierarchy of law derived ultimately from</p>\n<p>God in which positive law was worthy for its own sake10. It’s upon Aquinas</p>\n<p>discernment that natural law could be added to positive law as far as the first</p>\n<p>principles were concerned not subtracted from, thus development of natural law.</p>\n<p>Concept of natural law according to Thomas Aquinas. (1225- 1274)- discussed</p>\n<p>supra.</p>\n<p>Acquinas notes that;</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-law-and-morality-are-not-separable\">1. Law and morality are not separable</h3>\n<h3 id=\"2-all-laws-should-be-in-line-with-natural-law\">2. All laws should be in line with natural law</h3>\n<h3 id=\"3-law-is-devine-and-created-by-god\">3. Law is Devine and created by God</h3>\n<h3 id=\"4-interprets-law-as-part-of-theological-development\">4. Interprets law as part of theological development</h3>\n<p>Lastly, Aquinas defined five ‘Primary Precepts’ which detail the steps of leading a</p>\n<p>moral life; to seek self-preservation; to reproduce; to nurture offspring through</p>\n<p>education; to live in society; and to worship God.</p>\n<p>9</p>\n<p>Political theories of middle Ages (ed. Maitland) pp.9 et. Esp and 73 et esq.</p>\n<p>10</p>\n<p>See. T. Gilby, principality and polity, PP. 146 et Seq.</p>\n<p>17</p>\n<p>Modern natural law/scholastic era (110-1400) “classical natural law era”</p>\n<p>This was advanced between 18th and 20th century. The 18th century was referred to as</p>\n<p>the “age of reason” where secularism and rationalism held sway and the natural</p>\n<p>law in general came into existence. These classical natural law scholars had different</p>\n<p>intellectual disagreements with the ancient ones as regards what natural law means but</p>\n<p>still agreed to a larger extent. This particular stage was influenced by a number of</p>\n<p>events at the time which among others included the following;</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-the-industrial-revolution-which-led-to-growth-in-commodity-production-and\">1. The industrial revolution which led to growth in commodity production and</h3>\n<p>gave birth to merchant classes which was inferior in status but gradually</p>\n<p>became richer and stronger and inevitably gained influence in society.</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-dissolution-of-feudalism-people-become-less-attached-to-land-as-a-diversion\">2. Dissolution of feudalism. People become less attached to land as a diversion</h3>\n<p>from feudalism this because many had become merchants and there was no</p>\n<p>need of being tied onto land as a means of livelihood.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-development-at-both-national-and-international-level-capitalism-after-the\">3. Development at both national and international level. Capitalism after the</h3>\n<p>collapse of feudalism led to development of nation states which called for</p>\n<p>creation of governments.</p>\n<h3 id=\"4-separation-of-law-from-theology-the-above-mentioned-developments-all\">4. Separation of law from theology. The above-mentioned developments all</h3>\n<p>triggered the collapse of the feudal system which elevated the standards of</p>\n<p>middle-class business men who later began advocating for people’s rights.</p>\n<p>The 18th Century thinkers included; Immanuel Kant, David Hume, Montesquieu</p>\n<p>and Edmond Burke among others. However, the above thinkers did not reject</p>\n<p>natural law principles but modified them. These started viewing both economic rights</p>\n<p>and civil rights as part and parcel of natural law. With the continuation of their</p>\n<p>advocacy many revolutions such as the American and French revolutions were</p>\n<p>witnessed.</p>\n<p>18</p>\n<p>The classical/ modern natural law school had the following key elements;</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-it-was-secular-and-radical-in-nature\">1. It was secular and radical in nature</h3>\n<h3 id=\"2-law-played-a-paramount-role-as-compared-to-religion\">2. Law played a paramount role as compared to religion</h3>\n<h3 id=\"3-jurists-played-a-central-role-in-developing-natural-law-concepts\">3. Jurists played a central role in developing natural law concepts</h3>\n<p>The philosophers of this stage applied reasoning and rationality, they separated law</p>\n<p>and theology.</p>\n<p>Niccolomo Machiavelli [1469-1527].</p>\n<p>Italian diplomat, politician, philosopher and writer who authored a book “the</p>\n<p>prince”. In his book he illustrated how monarchies should rule where he clearly</p>\n<p>stated that “a prince never lacks legitimate reasons to break his promise” this</p>\n<p>clearly showed that to him a leader wasn’t answerable to his subjects and he had the</p>\n<p>authority to do whatever he wished as long as it is in his interest. This was the</p>\n<p>opposite as before during the ancient era a leader had to be honest, God fearing and</p>\n<p>truthful to his subjects. The prince indicates that indeed a ruler should have authority</p>\n<p>and that determines his power and as well states that laws are only meaningful if</p>\n<p>enforced. Therefore, a ruler should command an army. He states that its better to be</p>\n<p>feared than loved. He further states that the ruler should not depend or rely on</p>\n<p>people’s admiration, he only has a duty to make sure that he is obeyed. In his book</p>\n<p>still Machiavelli states that a ruler must to do anything to stay in power even though</p>\n<p>he doesn’t support violence. Machiavelli as opposed to the ancient natural law</p>\n<p>scholars he puts it straight that a ruler should do everything to make sure everything</p>\n<p>goes his way and not put faith in God that things will somehow workout.</p>\n<p>“Another reason to carry a big stick: No one respects weak people.” Machiavelli</p>\n<p>quotes…</p>\n<p>Hugo Grotius [1583-1645]</p>\n<p>Hugo Grotius was Dutch jurist and thinker considered to be the father of</p>\n<p>international law and never believed in war but in the “international</p>\n<p>19</p>\n<p>societal doctrine.” he believed that international conflicts can be resolved through</p>\n<p>actual laws and agreements other than warfare. He wanted to build a systematic</p>\n<p>system to avoid violence and warfare.</p>\n<p>He asserts that natural law is based on the nature of man and that to discover what</p>\n<p>natural law is, we must see what is in accordance with the rational and social nature</p>\n<p>of man and then test our conclusion by seeing whether a rule has been adopted by</p>\n<p>all nations or at least the most civilized. He indicates that indeed natural law is the</p>\n<p>parent of all laws. He further asserts that natural law entails principles of fairness</p>\n<p>hence calls for abstinence from what belongs to others, restoration or compensation,</p>\n<p>repayment and inflicting of fair punishments where one goes wrong. He lastly defined</p>\n<p>natural law as “the dictate of right of reason”</p>\n<p>Immanuel Kant (1724-1804) argued that morality arose from freedom and that when</p>\n<p>law is in conformity with morality one can be forced to obey the law without</p>\n<p>forfeiting freedom. His departure from the natural law was in his argument that</p>\n<p>freedom meant autonomy without no subjection to any determination other than</p>\n<p>one’s self.</p>\n<p>Baron de Montesquieu (1689-1755)11. He had no doubt that ‘the world of civil</p>\n<p>society has certainly been made by men’’.12 For Montesquieu mankind was influenced</p>\n<p>by a variety of factors; climate, religion, laws maxims of government, morals and</p>\n<p>customs. He sees an individual as merely an instrument of historical change.</p>\n<p>Natural law under the enlignment era</p>\n<p>The eighteenth (18) century was also a period of the Scottish enlightment13. Hume</p>\n<p>(1711-76), Adam Smith (1723-90)14 All this were group of intellectuals dedicated to</p>\n<p>study society scientifically. Hume</p>\n<p>11</p>\n<p>See the spirit of laws (1748). Montesquieu and natural law see M. Zucket (2001) 18 soc.</p>\n<p>Phil &amp; Pol. 227</p>\n<p>12</p>\n<p>The New science) ed) Bargin and Frisch) (1948) (originally published 1725), S. 331</p>\n<p>13</p>\n<p>The enlightment; An interpretation (1967)-1970. On Scotland see A chitris</p>\n<p>14</p>\n<p>On the contribution to jurisprudence of whom see D.D Raphael, Adam Smith (1985)</p>\n<p>20</p>\n<p>developed a modern theory of natural law,15 an exclusively empirical version of the</p>\n<p>fundamental principles of natural law. He argued that an invention of a naturally</p>\n<p>inventive species, and from that point of view ‘natural; which is a s old as the</p>\n<p>species.16 Hume called the rules of justice natural laws since they are as old and</p>\n<p>universal as society and the human species but prior to government and positive law.17</p>\n<p>David Humes [1711-1779]</p>\n<p>Humes urges that the modern theory of natural law ought to be exclusive from</p>\n<p>religion and should be dependent of the science of morality and law. He also asserts</p>\n<p>that laws of justice aren’t created by government but rather are inherent and universal</p>\n<p>in nature. He argues that these principles natural laws of justice existed even before</p>\n<p>governments were created.</p>\n<p>During the 19th century, natural law was not very influential because it was manned</p>\n<p>by materialism and questions of law, state and sovereignty were dominated by</p>\n<p>positivism. This led to the discovery that, laws of social order would be explained as</p>\n<p>the laws of state and it was dominated by scientific thought which affected both laws</p>\n<p>and social sciences, hence, the development of Marxism or historical materialism. The</p>\n<p>19th century did not envisage much of natural law but rather other theories however, it</p>\n<p>was revived during the 20th century.</p>\n<p>The 20th century attracted several natural law writers from the beginning to date.</p>\n<p>These included scholars like; Lon fuller, HLA Hart and John Finnis.</p>\n<p>H.L.A Hart though being a normative positivist, his ideas are relevant as far as he</p>\n<p>attempts to marry positivist ideas and natural law ideas. Hart made three (3) major</p>\n<p>prepositions in attempt to describe what natural law. He stated that, ‘Law is a social</p>\n<p>fact, law and morals are conceptually separable and that, law consist of the union of</p>\n<p>primary and secondary “social rules”.</p>\n<p>15</p>\n<p>By D. Forbes, Hume’s philosophical politics (1975), chaps</p>\n<p>16</p>\n<p>Ibid, P 70</p>\n<p>17</p>\n<p>ibid</p>\n<p>21</p>\n<p>In reference to social rules, He argued that, there are certain substantive rules which</p>\n<p>are essential of human beings are to live continuously together in close proximity.</p>\n<p>Such rules include, Human vulnerability, approximate equality, Limited altruism</p>\n<p>(concern for other people’s welfare needs above oneself or one’s own interest) limited</p>\n<p>resources, limited understanding and strength of will. Therefore, Hart saw no</p>\n<p>necessary link between law and morals.</p>\n<p>John Finnis in his book’ Natural law and natural rights tried to revive and reinstate</p>\n<p>natural law concepts. According to Finnis, natural law is set of principles of practical</p>\n<p>reasonableness in ordering human life and community.</p>\n<p>In his case, he identified seven (7) basic goals/ basic values which every reasonable</p>\n<p>person must assert for purposes of human survival and these include; Life which</p>\n<p>must be protected, knowledge or preference of truth over false hood, love for arts,</p>\n<p>Aesthetic experience for appreciation of beauty, being social with other people,</p>\n<p>practical reasonableness by use of intelligence to effectively deal with social problems</p>\n<p>and religion which he meant interest in the origin and purpose of life which if</p>\n<p>expressed as above is cosmic order and acceptable.</p>\n<p>Unique features or characteristics of natural law</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-natural-law-is-universal-unchanging-and-everlasting\">1. Natural law is universal unchanging and everlasting.</h3>\n<h3 id=\"2-whatever-is-good-is-in-accordance-with-nature-but-that-which-is-evil-is-contrary\">2. Whatever is good is in accordance with nature but that which is evil is contrary</h3>\n<p>to natural and therefore, natural law is good.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-there-exists-an-order-in-natural-which-is-rational-and-which-can-be-known-by\">3. There exists an order in natural which is rational and which can be known by</h3>\n<p>man.</p>\n<h3 id=\"4-whatever-exists-is-in-the-order-of-nature-cosmic-order-according-to-aquinas\">4. Whatever exists is in the order of nature (cosmic order). According to Aquinas,</h3>\n<p>natural represents man’s participation in the cosmic order with the aid of the</p>\n<p>power God has put in man.</p>\n<h3 id=\"5-natural-law-is-usually-used-synonymously-with-morality-where-both-coincide\">5. Natural law is usually used synonymously with morality. Where both coincide,</h3>\n<p>such synonymous usage is justified and where they deviate</p>\n<p>22</p>\n<p>or relating to only person’s social or moral way of life, it should be understood</p>\n<p>as not attributing it to natural law.</p>\n<h3 id=\"6-natural-law-essential-focuses-on-the-law-as-it-ought-to-be-a-law-whose-existence\">6. Natural law essential focuses on the law as it ought to be a law whose existence</h3>\n<p>is proved by reference to transcendental, idealistic, theological argument.</p>\n<p>Natural law therefore, reflects the order of nature and represents a blue print</p>\n<p>for decent orderly existence of humanity. According to Aquinas, every law</p>\n<p>should be just and he stated that, law to be just should move together with</p>\n<p>both values and morals.</p>\n<p>Relevance of natural law</p>\n<p>In critical analysis of the Natural law theory, it’s paramount to note down its</p>\n<p>influence, relevance and application in the judicial system of Uganda and</p>\n<p>communities. these relevancies include; -</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-the-natural-law-concept-is-of-relevance-to-natural-individuals-rights-of-uganda\">1. The natural law concept is of relevance to natural individuals’ rights of Uganda.</h3>\n<p>Forexample, the 1995 constitution of the Republic of Uganda as amended</p>\n<p>includes bill of rights enshrined under chapter 4 which recognizes the</p>\n<p>fundamental freedom and rights of people. The constitution further provides</p>\n<p>that fundamental rights and freedoms of individuals are inherent and not</p>\n<p>granted by the state. Such rights and freedoms of individuals and groups</p>\n<p>enshrined in this chapter shall be protected upheld and promoted by all organs</p>\n<p>of state18</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-the-theory-seeks-universality-and-commonality-in-human-law-values-and\">2. The theory seeks universality and commonality in human law, values and</h3>\n<p>institutions across the global. This meets the standards of United Nations</p>\n<p>which tends to bear laws for the protection of human rights and environmental</p>\n<p>law among other which are ratified by government pursuant to Article 123 of</p>\n<p>the 1995 constitution which allows Uganda to ratify treaties and conventions</p>\n<p>for instance,</p>\n<p>18</p>\n<p>Article 20 of the 1995 constitution of the Republic of Uganda as amended.</p>\n<p>23</p>\n<p>Universal declaration of Human rights (UDHR), the African charter were</p>\n<p>ratified by Uganda among other states.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-natural-law-emphasizes-the-need-for-legal-system-to-have-moral-goals-and\">3. Natural law emphasizes the need for legal system to have moral goals and</h3>\n<p>values although the definition of moral differs/ varies from place to place for</p>\n<p>different people, it’s still relevant in Uganda. This relevance is reflected in the</p>\n<p>fact that Uganda penalizes acts like homosexuality, bestiality, adultery.</p>\n<p>forexmaple, under section 4 of divorce Act, Adultery is one of the grounds</p>\n<p>of divorce in family law.</p>\n<h3 id=\"4-the-social-contact-arm-of-natural-law-advances-the-concept-of-representative\">4. The social contact arm of natural law advances the concept of representative</h3>\n<p>system of governance which is in practice in Uganda today making the theory</p>\n<p>applicable. The constitution states that sovereignty belongs to the people who</p>\n<p>shall exercise their sovereignty in accordance with this constitution because all</p>\n<p>the authority emanates from the people of Uganda and people shall be</p>\n<p>governed through their will and consent19. In furtherance of the above, the</p>\n<p>constitution provides that every person above eighteen (18) years has a</p>\n<p>right to vote despite the mistreatments envisaged during this exercise20</p>\n<h3 id=\"5-the-notion-of-rule-of-law-was-further-developed-from-the-natural-school\">5. The notion of rule of law was further developed from the natural school.</h3>\n<p>Under this doctrine, the rules provide for equality of all persons before the</p>\n<p>law21. In the case of Hon. Karuhanga Gerald Vs Attorney General22. Rule</p>\n<p>of law was defined to mean a doctrine where people are below the law. This</p>\n<p>notion is reflected in the Uganda judicial system despite criticisms and abuse by</p>\n<p>leaders hence failure to observe the rule of law.</p>\n<h3 id=\"6-customary-law-is-recognised-in-the-natural-law-school-and-has-been-regarded-as\">6. Customary law is recognised in the natural law school and has been regarded as</h3>\n<p>major source of law in Uganda. Uganda recognizes customary laws and the</p>\n<p>norms which are in line with Article 2 of the 1995 constitution as source</p>\n<p>of law and 23. In the case of</p>\n<p>19</p>\n<p>Article 1 of the 1995 constitution of the Republic of Uganda as amended.</p>\n<p>20</p>\n<p>Article 59 of the 1995 constitution of Republic of Uganda as amended.</p>\n<p>21</p>\n<p>Article 21 of the 1995 constitution of Republic of Uganda as amended.</p>\n<p>22</p>\n<p>Constitutional petition 2014.</p>\n<p>23</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-15-of-judicature-act-cap-13\">Section 15 of Judicature Act. Cap 13.</h3>\n<p>24</p>\n<p>Bruno Kiwuwa Vs Ivan Serunkuma &amp; Juliet Namazzi24. where court</p>\n<p>recognized a Kiganda culture of not marrying in the same clan and it defined</p>\n<p>customs as practice that has been followed in particular locality in such</p>\n<p>circumstance that it has to be accepted as part of the law of that locality.</p>\n<h3 id=\"7-natural-proponents-also-propounded-that-some-rights-should-be-limited-the\">7. Natural proponents also propounded that some rights should be limited. The</h3>\n<p>constitution of the Republic of Uganda Limits certain rights like freedom of</p>\n<p>movements, liberty, speech.25 For example Uganda restricted and declared</p>\n<p>lockdown where people were not allowed to move to prevent the spread of</p>\n<p>Covid -19 and Ebola in Uganda.</p>\n<h3 id=\"8-the-notion-of-natural-law-has-enhanced-for-the-existence-of-doctrine-of\">8. The notion of natural law has enhanced for the existence of doctrine of</h3>\n<p>separation of powers which is reflected in the constitution of Uganda where eth</p>\n<p>three (3) arms of government Executive, judiciary and legislature are required</p>\n<p>to work independently despite several manipulations. These are provided</p>\n<p>under chapter 7,8 and 6 respectively.</p>\n<h3 id=\"9-the-values-attached-to-religions-by-naturalist-also-reflects-the-african-traditional\">9. The values attached to religions by naturalist also reflects the African traditional</h3>\n<p>jurisprudence. For instance, religion encourages humanity which is reflected in</p>\n<p>African Ubuntu bulamu (love your neighbor as you love yourself).</p>\n<h3 id=\"10-natural-law-theory-resulted-to-the-outbreak-of-revolutions-and-liberal\">10. Natural law theory resulted to the outbreak of revolutions and liberal</h3>\n<p>movements for example the 1986 coming into power of the natural Resistance</p>\n<p>Movement (NRM), the 1789 French revolution and American war of</p>\n<p>independence which impacted need for independence among African states for</p>\n<p>example. It influenced leaders like; Mandela (RIP) of south Africa, Nkwame</p>\n<p>Nkrumah of Ghana in 1957 to fight for their independence from the colonial</p>\n<p>and British government / Rule.</p>\n<p>24</p>\n<p>HCCS 52 of 2006.</p>\n<p>25</p>\n<p>Article 43 of the 1995 constitution as amended.</p>\n<p>25</p>\n<p>Criticisms to the natural law theory</p>\n<p>1) Two philosophers (Aquinas and Aristotle) integral to the theory have</p>\n<p>different views about God’s role in nature, which confuses the issue, especially</p>\n<p>when trying to decipher if the theory relies on the existence of God.</p>\n<p>2) Human behavior may be solely reliant upon the environment that one is</p>\n<p>exposed to, which includes social classes, education and upbringing, this</p>\n<p>opposes the theory since it clearly states that laws and rights are inherent in</p>\n<p>nature and universal.</p>\n<p>3) How do we determine the essential or morally praiseworthy traits of human</p>\n<p>nature? Traditional natural law theory has picked out very positive traits, such</p>\n<p>as &quot;the desire to know the truth, to choose the good, and to develop as healthy</p>\n<p>mature human beings”. But some philosophers, such as Hobbes, have found</p>\n<p>human beings to be essentially selfish. It is questionable that behavior in</p>\n<p>accordance with human nature is morally right and behavior not in accord with</p>\n<p>human nature is morally wrong. For instance, if it turns out that human beings</p>\n<p>(at least the males) are naturally aggressive, should we infer that war and</p>\n<p>fighting are morally, right?</p>\n<p>4) For Aquinas, the reason why nature had the order it did was because God had</p>\n<p>put it there. Other thinkers, such as Aristotle, did not believe that this order was</p>\n<p>divinely inspired. Does this alleged natural moral order require that we believe</p>\n<p>that there is a God that has produced this natural moral order? Evolutionary</p>\n<p>theory has challenged much of the basis of thinking that there is a moral</p>\n<p>natural order, since on evolutionary theory species has developed the way they</p>\n<p>have out of survival needs.</p>\n<p>26</p>\n<p>Conclusion</p>\n<p>The concept of Natural law is so relevant, appreciative and influential in the global</p>\n<p>setup and existence of nature (Cosmic order). While tracing its origin, it has positively</p>\n<p>impacted the global setup and governance despite certain abuses that have been</p>\n<p>registered by the leaders who assume leadership powers at the expense and violation</p>\n<p>of human rights in the communities as discussed above.</p>\n<p>Bibliography        /references</p>\n<p>Text books</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-lloyd-s-introduction-to-jurisprudence-eighteenth-edition\">1. Lloyd’s introduction to jurisprudence eighteenth edition</h3>\n<h3 id=\"2-omony-john-paul-key-issues-to-jurisprudence\">2. Omony John Paul- Key issues to jurisprudence</h3>\n<h3 id=\"3-axley-r-a-defence-of-radbruch-s-formula-from-david-dyzenhaus-ed\">3. Axley, R,” A defence of Radbruch’s formula” from David Dyzenhaus (ed)</h3>\n<p>Recrafting the rule of law: The limits of legal order (1999), Hart publishing.</p>\n<p>27</p>\n<h3 id=\"4-hart-hla-definition-and-theory-in-jurisprudence-1954-70-lqr-37\">4. Hart HLA “Definition and theory in Jurisprudence” (1954) 70 LQR 37.</h3>\n<p>(Reprinted by kind permission of the author and the editor of law quarterly</p>\n<p>review, Stevens &amp; sons Ltd)</p>\n<p>Statutes</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-the-1995-constitution-of-the-republic-of-uganda-as-amended\">1. The 1995 constitution of the Republic of Uganda as amended</h3>\n<h3 id=\"2-divorce-act\">2. Divorce Act</h3>\n<h3 id=\"3-judicature-act-cap-13\">3. Judicature Act cap 13</h3>\n<h3 id=\"4-case-laws\">4. Case laws</h3>\n<p>28</p>";
